[center][size=5][b]Mod Skyrim by talking to your AI assistant[/b][/size][/center]

[size=5][b]Description[/b][/size]
[b]Hi, fellow modders![/b] I love this community, and I enjoy your mods every day. Modding Skyrim in my youth is what finally made me become a developer, so this project is my way of giving something back. I hope it makes modding a little easier, and a lot more fun, for you too.

Skyrim Modding Tools is an [b]MCP server[/b]: a set of tools an AI assistant can use on your own Skyrim Special Edition setup. It works with [b]Claude Code, Claude Desktop, VS Code, ChatGPT[/b] and any other MCP client, [b]local models[/b] on your own PC included, and with [b]Mod Organizer 2[/b] on Windows or [b]Amethyst Mod Manager[/b] on Linux.

Ask it things like:
[quote]Make me a sword called Flame Blade that sets enemies on fire.
Which mods change the Iron Sword, and which one wins?
Make a patch that makes the Iron Sword a bit stronger.
Put a chest in Breezehome holding 500 gold and a Steel Sword.
Add a dragon shout that deals shock damage, with three words of its own.
Open Breezehome in Blender. I want to move the furniture around.
Make the navmesh for my new house, so my follower can walk in.
Make me a small valley from this heightmap, with a lake at the bottom.
My game just crashed. Why?[/quote]

A mod is made as text, one file per record, and built into a real plugin: weapons, armor, arrows, potions, recipes, enchantments, spells, shouts, NPCs, leveled lists, perks, notes, quests with their scripts, and whatever is placed in the world, indoors and out. The build warns of what would go wrong in game, and each warning comes with the one step that fixes it.

[size=4][b]Always the newest version[/b][/size]
This page has one file, the installer, and it never changes: it installs the newest version of the server straight from GitHub. To update, run it again. You never need to download anything here twice. What changed lately is in the [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-mcp/commits/main]server's history[/url] and the [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-core/tags]releases of the tools[/url] on GitHub.

[size=4][b]Modding with Muddy![/b][/size]
[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-mcp/main/docs/nexus/muddy.png[/img][/center]
Meet [b]Muddy[/b], the modding agent: a mudcrab from the shore below Riverwood who learned to mod Skyrim. Cheerful, proud of his shell, and exact at the workbench. He reads the modding guides before he writes a record, builds until the build says it worked, and leaves your mod manager alone unless you ask.
[list]
[*][b]Claude Code, VS Code, Claude Desktop, ChatGPT and others:[/b] the setup asks if you want him and sets him up. The [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-mcp/blob/main/docs/Muddy.md]guide to Muddy[/url] has the steps for each one.
[*][b]OpenClaw:[/b] the OpenClaw plugin's wizard, [code]openclaw skyrim setup[/code], makes him an agent and asks which model he thinks with.
[*][b]The simplest way:[/b] copy his two files, AGENTS.md and SOUL.md, into the folder your AI works in.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Made with AI, openly[/b][/size]
The code was written together with Claude (Anthropic), and the banner was generated with FLUX. That's why this page carries the [i]AI-Generated Content[/i] and [i]AI Media[/i] tags. It is tested: well over a thousand automated tests, on Windows and Linux, and regular evaluation rounds in which AI models, small local ones too, build real mods on a test game, each checked in the plugin it built.

[size=4][b]Source and license[/b][/size]
Free and open source, under GPL-3.0:
[list]
[*][url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-mcp]skyrim-modding-tools-mcp[/url]: this MCP server
