[size=5][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]
This is a program for your PC, not a mod for your game: download the main file manually, not with your mod manager.
[list=1]
[*]Download the main file, the installer, and unpack it anywhere.
[*][b]Windows:[/b] double-click [code]Install on Windows.cmd[/code]. If Windows asks whether to run it, choose Run (on a blue SmartScreen window: More info, then Run anyway).
[b]Linux:[/b] open a terminal in that folder and run [code]bash install-linux.sh[/code]
[*]The installer brings git and Node.js where they are missing, downloads the newest version of the server from GitHub into your user folder ([code]skyrim-modding-tools-mcp[/code]) and runs its setup. Setup finds your mod manager, builds everything, registers the server with Claude Code, Claude Desktop and VS Code, sets up Muddy, and ends with a health check. Both ask before they install anything.
[*]Restart your MCP client, and ask it something.
[/list]
[b]Updating:[/b] run the installer again. It updates the server where it is and keeps your settings and your projects. Then restart your MCP client.
Step-by-step instructions, and setting it up by hand, are on [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-mcp#setup]GitHub[/url].

[color=#e8a33d][b]Please start on a test instance.[/b][/color] Deploying and installing write into your Mod Organizer instance's mods and profile. A portable Mod Organizer on a clean game is the safe place to learn the tools. If your setup was installed by Wabbajack, its next update deletes every mod it did not install.

[size=5][b]Main features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Look it up:[/b] any record by name, editor ID or FormID, across your whole load order. Which mod adds it, which one wins, who else changes it.
[*][b]Make mods as text:[/b] a mod is a folder of plain YAML files in Spriggit's format, easy to read and to keep in git. The build checks every link, gives new records their FormIDs, compiles Papyrus scripts and writes a real plugin, then tells you what it holds.
[*][b]Warned before you play:[/b] an armor no one can wear, a missing mesh, an effect that does nothing, a chest under the floor or floating in the air. Every warning names the one step that fixes it.
[*][b]Patch without touching:[/b] copy another mod's record into your own patch and change it there. The original mod stays as it is.
[*][b]Items, magic, NPCs and more:[/b] weapons, armor, arrows and potions with their recipes and enchantments; spells, magic effects and shouts with words of their own; NPCs and followers; leveled lists for loot and merchants; perks; quests with their Papyrus scripts.
[*][b]Give NPCs new lines:[/b] a topic the player can ask and what an NPC answers, given to that NPC alone. The build writes the SEQ file a quest's dialogue needs, and warns where nobody, or everybody, would say a line.
[*][b]Change how the game plays:[/b] game settings such as jump height or prices, globals, and the keywords the game checks on an item.
[*][b]Place things in the world:[/b] put a chest, a lamp or an NPC in a house or outdoors, beside what the game already places there. Warned when it would float, sink into the ground or stand inside something. Arrange a cell in Blender, by hand or through Blender's MCP server, and bring it back.
[*][b]A room of your own:[/b] a new interior made like the game's rooms, with its walls, lights and furniture, and a pair of load doors to the cell the player comes from.
[*][b]Navmesh in one call, no Creation Kit:[/b] a cell's navmesh made from what stands in it, indoors and outdoors, linked to the cells beside it, so NPCs and followers walk around what you placed. Touch it up in Blender if you like. The build tells you when a cell's navmesh no longer fits what you changed there.
[*][b]Shape the land:[/b] flatten, raise, lower or smooth the ground outdoors, or sculpt it in Blender and bring it back.
[*][b]A land of your own:[/b] a new worldspace, flat or shaped by a heightmap image, with water and Skyrim's sky and weather. Type [code]coc[/code] in the console to go there, then build on it like anywhere else.
[*][b]Find and settle mod conflicts:[/b] which plugin's changes to a record are lost to another's, change by change, and which mod's files hide another's, loose or in archives. One call makes a patch that keeps every plugin's changes; plugins and mods can be moved in the order on your word.
[*][b]Sort the load order:[/b] LOOT's own library sorts your plugins and shows what LOOT says about them: what would move, dirty plugins, missing masters. You see it first; it is applied only when you say so.
[*][b]Install mods:[/b] search Nexus Mods, read a mod's requirements, install it with its FOMOD installer answered, list, enable or disable your mods, and check SKSE plugins against your game version.
[*][b]Read crash logs:[/b] Crash Logger SSE's logs, checked against over a hundred known crashes, with the mod behind every file and record the log names.
[*][b]Read the Papyrus log:[/b] what your scripts did wrong, grouped and counted, each script traced to its mod, with script lag and what your save still holds of removed mods. No crash is put on it: it is no crash log.
[*][b]Game settings files:[/b] Skyrim.ini, SkyrimPrefs.ini and SkyrimCustom.ini changed on your word.
[*][b]The know-how built in:[/b] modding guides for every kind of record, plugins and FormIDs, load order and conflicts, game updates and crash analysis, which your AI reads before it starts.
[/list]
Some work still needs other tools: a new NPC's face needs the Creation Kit, distant land needs LOD from xLODGen. What you make in the Creation Kit, the tools read back into the project's text.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Skyrim Special Edition 1.5.97 to 1.7.x
[*]Mod Organizer 2 (2.5) on Windows, or Amethyst Mod Manager (2.5) on Linux
[*][url=https://nodejs.org]Node.js 24[/url] and [url=https://git-scm.com]git[/url], which the installer brings where they are missing. The setup installs the rest: the .NET 10 SDK, and on Linux a Papyrus compiler.
[*]An MCP client: Claude Code, Claude Desktop, VS Code, ChatGPT and others. For OpenClaw, there is [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/skyrim-modding-tools-openclaw-plugin]the OpenClaw plugin[/url].
[*]Optional: the Creation Kit (free on Steam), to compile Papyrus scripts. On Windows, its compiler needs .NET Framework 3.5 (Windows Features).
[*]Optional: Blender with the [url=https://github.com/BadDogSkyrim/PyNifly]PyNifly[/url] add-on, to arrange a cell, its ground or its navmesh in Blender. On Linux, its [url=https://github.com/Ciltress/PyNifly]Linux build[/url]. Land, navmesh and a new worldspace need no Blender.
[/list]
[b]Nexus Mods and your API key:[/b] searching Nexus and reading mod pages need no key. Downloading through Nexus's API needs the personal API key of a [b]premium[/b] account. The key stays on your PC and is sent only to Nexus's API. Without one, you download the file yourself, and the tools install it.

[size=5][b]Shout outs[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://github.com/Mutagen-Modding/Mutagen]Mutagen[/url] by Noggog, which reads and writes the plugins, and [url=https://github.com/Mutagen-Modding/Spriggit]Spriggit[/url], whose text format the projects use
[*]BadDog's [url=https://github.com/BadDogSkyrim/PyNifly]PyNifly[/url], which brings Skyrim's meshes into Blender and back
[*]Phostwood's [url=https://github.com/Phostwood/crash-analyzer]Skyrim Crash Log Analyzer[/url], whose crash rules the crash-log reader applies
[*]Arthmoor's notes on Papyrus log errors, DavidJCobb's Papyrus logging guide and Nightfallstorm's Papyrus Tweaks NG, which the Papyrus log reader follows
[*][url=https://github.com/recastnavigation/recastnavigation]Recast[/url] by Mikko Mononen, through ikpil's [url=https://github.com/ikpil/DotRecast]DotRecast[/url], which builds the navmeshes, and [url=https://github.com/ousnius/NiflySharp]NiflySharp[/url], which reads the meshes' collision
[*]The LOOT team's [url=https://github.com/loot/libloot]libloot[/url] and LOOT's masterlist, which sort the load order
[*]Everyone in this community who shares mods, tools, guides and knowledge
[/list]
Found a bug or have an idea? Tell me in the Bugs or Posts tab here, or open an issue on GitHub.

Happy modding! <3
